WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.875
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Are
you a developer who runs

00:00:01.875 --> 00:00:03.900
workloads on Kubernetes or GKE?

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.360
Well, if you are, then
you're probably familiar

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.180
with Prometheus, the de facto
standard for open source

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.920
monitoring on Kubernetes.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.950
And you probably know that
managing Prometheus at scale

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.470
can be a real big
challenge sometimes.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:17.370
My name is Yuri.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.050
I'm an SRE at Google Cloud.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.260
And today, I'm going to show
you how to use Google's Managed

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.660
Service for Prometheus
to monitor your GKE

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.210
workloads without having
to manually manage

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:28.380
Prometheus at scale.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.347
This is engineering
for reliability.

00:00:31.347 --> 00:00:34.568
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:34.568 --> 00:00:36.360
The first thing to
understand is the nature

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.830
of the service itself.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.110
This is Google Cloud's
fully Managed Service

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.640
for Prometheus metrics.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.610
It's built on top of Monarch,
the same globally-scalable data

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.085
store as Cloud monitoring.

00:00:46.085 --> 00:00:48.210
You can replace your existing
Prometheus deployment

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.280
to collect cluster
and workload metrics

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.970
and then query the data across
multiple clusters using PromQL.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.030
Sounds intriguing?

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:55.920
I'm so glad.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.770
Let's have a look at
how you can get started.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.330
Let's start by validating our
existing Prometheus deployment.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:06.330
Here, we can see that we have
all of the necessary components

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:06.840
running.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.120
This includes the
Prometheus server itself;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.520
the node exporter, which
collects infrastructure metrics

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.340
from our cluster nodes;
and the Grafana UI.

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.925
Next, let's make sure that
metric collection is working.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.820
We can connect to the Prometheus
server UI using port forwarding

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:28.520
and run a query to confirm that
metrics are being collected

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.710
and that we're
able to query them.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:33.170
At this point, we're ready
to migrate this installation

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.345
to send metrics to the
Managed Prometheus Service.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.750
The Prometheus operator is using
a Prometheus custom resource

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:43.890
to manage these components.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:49.790
We can edit it
and simply replace

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.913
the images using with
Google's forked version

00:01:51.913 --> 00:01:52.955
of the Prometheus server.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:01.750
This binary is configured
to scrape both workload

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.880
metrics and its own
metrics endpoint

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.490
and send the collected data to
the Managed Service backend.

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:14.830
Once we confirm that the
server has restarted,

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.990
let's again validate
local metric collection

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.910
and querying using
port forwarding.

00:02:27.007 --> 00:02:28.465
Everything still
works as expected.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.950
But now, we can go
to the Cloud console

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.680
and run our query there.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.530
This confirms that these metrics
are being ingested properly.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:43.470
We can also query the
same metrics using

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:45.480
Metrics Explorer, for
example, and use them

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:46.635
to create charts or alerts.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:00.450
So if you're already running
Prometheus in your GKE cluster,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:03.210
you can easily migrate to the
Google Cloud Managed Service

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:05.370
for Prometheus and
ingest your metrics

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.810
into Cloud monitoring just
by replacing the Prometheus

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:08.310
binary.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.800
It's just that easy.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.450
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.700
We learned how Google's
Managed Service for Prometheus

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.950
allows you to monitor your
clusters without having

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.690
to manage Prometheus
manually, how

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.670
to migrate your existing
Prometheus setup,

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.220
and how to use PromQL
to query the data.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.830
Next time, we'll instrument
our own application

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.080
and configure Prometheus
monitoring for it.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.450
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:33.180
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.060
See you soon.

